  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $\u2016u\u2016=4$ and $\u2016\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v\u2016=\\sqrt{182}.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $\u2016v\u2016.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $\\sqrt{2}u$ is orthogonal to $\\sqrt{3}v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{\u2016}^{2} & =\u2016\\sqrt{2}u{\u2016}^{2}+\u2016\\sqrt{3}v{\u2016}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{\u2016}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2}\u2016u\u2016)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\u2016v\u2016)^{2} \\\\ \u2016\\sqrt{2}u+\\sqrt{3}v{\u2016}^{2} & =2\u2016u{\u2016}^{2}+3\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ (\\sqrt{182})^{2} & =2(4)^{2}+3\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =2\\cdot 16+3\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ 182 & =32+3\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ 150 & =3\u2016v{\u2016}^{2} \\\\ \u2016v{\u2016}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\u2016v\u2016=5\\sqrt{2}.$",
